To add your own command to `bin/console` from a plugin, you need to register it as a service in your plugin's `src/Resources/config/services.php`. Here is an example of how to do this:

```php
// src/Command/ExampleCommand.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);
namespace Swag\BasicExample\Command;

use Symfony\Component\Console\Command\Command;
use Symfony\Component\Console\Input\InputInterface;
use Symfony\Component\Console\Output\OutputInterface;

class ExampleCommand extends Command {
    protected function execute(InputInterface $input, OutputInterface $output): int {
        $output->writeln('Hello from ExampleCommand');
        return Command::SUCCESS;
    }
}
```

To pass extra data from your plugin to a storefront pagelet like the footer and display it in Twig, you need to follow these steps:

1. **Subscribe to an event**: You need to subscribe to the `FooterPageletLoadedEvent` to add custom data when the Footer Pagelet is loaded.
2. **Register your subscriber**: Register your subscriber in the DI-Container with the tag `kernel.event_subscriber`.
3. **Add data to the page**: In your subscriber, you can then add the extra data to the footer object and pass it through the event parameters.
4. **Display the data in Twig**: Finally, extend the footer template and access the added data using a variable.

Here's an example of how you might implement this:

### Subscribe to an event
```php [PLUGIN_ROOT/src/Service/AddDataToPage.php]
namespace Swag\BasicExample\Service;
use Shopware\Storefront\Pagelet\Footer\FooterPageletLoadedEvent;
use Symfony\Component\EventDispatcher\EventSubscriberInterface;

class AddDataToPage implements EventSubscriberInterface {
    public static function getSubscribedEvents(): array
    {
        return [
            FooterPageletLoadedEvent::class => 'addActiveProductCount',
        ];
    }

    public function addActiveProductCount(FooterPageletLoadedEvent $event): void
    {
        // Add your custom data here, e.g., the count of active products.
        $footer = $event->getFooter();
        if ($footer) {
            $footerExtensions = $footer->getExtension('extensions') ?? [];
            $footerExtensions['product_count'] = ['count' => 10]; // Example data
            $footer->setExtension('extensions', $footerExtensions);
        }
    }
}
```

### Register your subscriber in the DI-Container
```php [PLUGIN_ROOT/Resources/config/services.php]
$services->set(
    \
Swag\BasicExample\Service\AddDataToPage::class\
)->tag('kernel.event_subscriber');
```

### Display the data in Twig
In your footer template, you can now access this data:
```twig [PLUGIN_ROOT/src/Resources/views/storefront/layout/footer/footer.html.twig]
{% sw_extends '@Storefront/storefront/layout/footer/footer.html.twig' %}

{% block layout_footer_navigation_columns %}
    {{ parent() }}

    {% if footer.extensions.product_count %}
        <div class="col-md-4 footer-column">
            <p>This shop offers you {{ footer.extensions.product_count.count }} products</p>
        </div>
    {% endif %}
{% endblock %}
```

This approach works both in plugins and apps. In plugins, you can also use the `StorefrontRenderEvent` to add custom data to the header and footer.

To define a custom entity for your own database table with the Data Abstraction Layer (DAL), you need to create an `EntityDefinition` class that extends from `Shopware\Core\Framework\DataAbstractionLayer\EntityDefinition`. This definition should be placed inside a folder named after the domain it handles. For example, if you were creating a custom entity for the 'Checkout' domain, your file would be located in `<plugin root>/src/Core/Content/Example/ExampleDefinition.php`.

Here's an example of how to define a simple `ExampleDefinition` class:

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Core/Content/Example/ExampleDefinition.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1); namespace Swag\BasicExample\Core\Content\Example;
use Shopware\Core\Framework\DataAbstractionLayer\EntityDefinition;
use Shopware\Core\Framework\DataAbstractionLayer\FieldCollection;

class ExampleDefinition extends EntityDefinition
{
    public const ENTITY_NAME = 'swag_example';

    public function getEntityName(): string
    {
        return self::ENTITY_NAME;
    }

    protected function defineFields(): FieldCollection
    {
        return new FieldCollection([]);
    }
}
```

To fetch products by name using the product repository, you can create a `Criteria` object and add an `EqualsFilter` for the `name` field. Here's how you can do it:

```php
public function readData(Context $context): void
{
    $criteria = new Criteria();
    $criteria->addFilter(new EqualsFilter('name', 'Example name'));

    $products = $this->productRepository->search($criteria, $context);
}
```
This example will search for all products with the name `Example name` and return an `EntitySearchResult` containing all matched products. You can then iterate over the results using a `foreach`. All available fields can be found in the entities' respective definition, such as `ProductDefinition` for this example.

You can find more examples of how to use filters in our [DAL reference about filters](../../../../development/troubleshooting/dal-reference/filters-reference.md).

To filter the association of productReviews where each review has a points value greater than or equal to 4, you can use `getAssociation` method and add a filter as shown in the following code snippet:

```php
public function readData(Context $context): void {
    // This will only return products that have at least one matching review with a points value of 4 or more.
    $criteria = new Criteria();
    $criteria->addFilter(new EqualsFilter('name', 'Example name'));
    $criteria->getAssociation('productReviews')->addFilter(new RangeFilter('points', [
        RangeFilter::GTE => 4
    ]));
    $products = $this->productRepository->search($criteria, $context)->first();
}
```

To create or update an entity using the `upsert` method, you need to provide the entity name and a payload which includes the ID of the existing entity. Here's how you can do it:

```twig
{% do services.writer.upsert('product', [
    { 'id': '<your product ID here>', 'name': 'New Name' }
]) %}
```

In this example, if a `product` with the specified ID already exists, it will be updated. If not, a new entity will be created.

For more details and examples, refer to [this section](https://developer.shopware.com/docs/resources/references/app-reference/script-reference/custom-endpoint-script-services-reference.html#upsert) of the documentation.

To compute the average review rating of products using a DAL aggregation, you can use an `AvgAggregation` on the 'rating' field. Here's how you can set it up in PHP Criteria:

```php
criteria = new Criteria();
criteria->addAggregation(new AvgAggregation('avg-rating', 'rating'));

result = $repository->search($criteria, $context);

/** @var AvgResult $aggregation */
$aggregation = $result->getAggregations()->get('avg-rating');

$aggregation->getAvg();
```
or in API Criteria:

```json
"aggregations": [
 {
        "name": "avg-rating",
        "type": "avg",
        "field": "rating"
 }
]
```
This will calculate the average of all numeric values for the 'rating' field.

