To create your own Store API endpoint in a plugin, follow these steps based on the provided documentation:

1. **Create a route class** that extends `AbstractExampleRoute` and define your route using the `#[Route]` attribute.
2. Use named routes internally by defining the route path with the `path` attribute.
3. Ensure each route class or API method requires the attribute: `#[Route(defaults: [|Shopware\Core\PlatformRequest::ATTRIBUTE_ROUTE_SCOPE| => [|Shopware\Core\Framework\Routing\StoreApiRouteScope::ID|]])]`.
4. Implement the logic in your route class, such as fetching data from repositories and returning it through a `StoreApiResponse`.
5. Use specific directory conventions for app scripts to register API endpoints, like `/store-api/script/` for Store API routes.
6. For Storefront integration, ensure controllers call Store API routes instead of accessing repositories directly.
7. Install your plugin using the Shopware CLI and test your endpoint with a tool like Postman or Insomnia.

Here's an example of what your route class might look like:

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Core/Content/Example/SalesChannel/ExampleRoute.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1)=>
namespace Swag\BasicExample\Core\Content\Example\SalesChannel;
use Shopware\Core\Framework\Routing\StoreApiRouteScope;
use Shopware\Core\PlatformRequest;
use Shopware\Core\DataAbstractionLayer\EntityRepository;
use Shopware\Core\System\SalesChannel\SalesChannelContext;

#[Route(defaults: [PlatformRequest::ATTRIBUTE_ROUTE_SCOPE => [StoreApiRouteScope::ID]])]
class ExampleRoute extends AbstractExampleRoute
{
    protected EntityRepository $exampleRepository;

    public function __construct(EntityRepository $exampleRepository)
    {
        $this->exampleRepository = $exampleRepository;
    }

    #[Route(path: '/store-api/example', name: 'store-api.example.search', methods: ['GET','POST'], defaults: ['_entity' => 'swag_example'])]
    public function load(Criteria $criteria, SalesChannelContext $context): ExampleRouteResponse
    {
        return new ExampleRouteResponse($this->exampleRepository->search($criteria, $context->getContext()));
    }
}
```

This example shows how to create a route that fetches data from an entity repository and returns it as a `StoreApiResponse`. Adjust the logic according to your specific needs.

To change the cart at runtime and add a custom discount line item, you can use the `discount()` function in your app script. Here's how you can achieve this:

```twig
{% set discountPrice = services.cart.price.create(
    'default' => { 'gross': 19.99, 'net': 19.99 },
    'EUR'   => { 'gross': 19.99, 'net': 19.99 }
) %}

{% if not services.cart.has('my-custom-discount') %}
    {% do services.cart.discount('my-custom-discount', 'absolute', discountPrice, 'my.custom.discount.label'|trans) %}
{% endif %}
```

This code snippet creates a price definition for the discount and checks if the discount is already added. If not, it adds the custom discount to the cart.

You can also remove an existing line item using `remove()` function like this:

```twig
{# first add the product #}
{% do services.cart.products.add(productId) %}
{# then remove it again #}
{% do services.cart.remove(productId) %}

{# first add the discount #}
{% do services.cart.discount('my-custom-discount', 'percentage', 10, 'A custom 10% discount') %}
{# then remove it again #}
{% do services.cart.remove('my-custom-discount') %}
```

This will add a product and the discount to the cart, and then remove both.

For more details on manipulating the cart with app scripts, refer to [Manipulate the Cart with App Scripts](https://developer.shopware.com/docs/guides/plugins/apps/app-scripts/cart-manipulation#line-items).

Remember that changing prices directly is not recommended unless absolutely necessary. Use discounts or surcharges for better practices.
